    <h1>What to Bring</h1>
  <h3>Essential Gear</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Sturdy footwear: Sneakers with good tread or lightweight hiking shoes.</p></li>
      <li><p>Navigation: A downloaded map of the trail or a screenshot of the trailhead marker.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Hydration and Nutrition</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><p>At least 16–32 ounces of water, especially during hot summer months.</p></li>
      <li><p>Quick Snacks: Energy bars, trail mix, or fruit for longer loops.</p></li>
      <li><p>Pet Water: A collapsible bowl and extra water.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Weather & Sun Protection</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Sunscreen: SPF 30 or higher.</p></li>
      <li><p>Hat & Sunglasses: To shade your eyes and face from glare.</p></li>
      <li><p>Light Layers: A windbreaker or light jacket for early morning or evening temperature drops.</p></li>
      <li><p>Bug Spray: Insect repellent, especially for people that are thinking of going on the dirt trails.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Personal Items & Tools</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Cell Phone: Fully charged for emergencies, photos, and navigation.</p></li>
      <li><p>First Aid Basics: Adhesive bandages, antiseptic wipes, and tweezers for splinters or ticks.</p></li>
      <li><p>Trash Bag: A small grocery bag to pack out your own garbage and keep the trail clean.</p></li>
      <li><p>Bags for Waste: Dog waste bags to clean up after pets.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </body>
</html>

